1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,000
Why does a coffee shop lose 2.2% in fees and wait two days when you swipe a card, but receives 100% of your 50,000 VND in three seconds when you scan a QR code?

2
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:18,510
A card swipe is a pull payment: the card issuer fronts money for 45 days and charges a 2.2% merchant fee to cover credit default risk.

3
00:00:18,510 --> 00:00:23,000
A QR code is a push payment: you are directly transferring cash you already own.

4
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:36,000
In three seconds, your bank debits your checking balance, the national switch routes the message, and the shop's bank immediately credits their account at T+0 under central-bank settlement guarantees.

5
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:40,240
Commercial banks do not shuttle tiny 50,000 VND amounts back and forth all day.

6
00:00:40,240 --> 00:00:49,000
At scheduled cut-off windows, the switch calculates a single multilateral net position, moving only the net difference across reserve accounts at the central bank.

7
00:00:49,000 --> 00:01:00,000
Banks provide free QR transfers because it anchors immense pools of low-cost CASA checking deposits paying roughly 0.1% interest, which banks re-lend into profitable corporate loans at 8%.

8
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:04,570
At local counters, scan QR codes to help merchants keep 100% of their earnings.

9
00:01:04,570 --> 00:01:10,000
For unfamiliar online shopping, use credit cards to retain vital chargeback protection rights.
